From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lagz0ne <15101444+lagz0ne@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 08:22:57 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 58/60] perf(previews): moving or renaming a frame keeps its
 picture

A frame's picture was current only while frame.updatedAt matched the version
it was drawn from. store.updateFrame bumps updatedAt on every write (a move,
a rename, a lock, a restack, an approval), so after a drag the editor asked
for the new version, the stored picture read as old, and the request waited
for Chromium to draw the same pixels again (0.3-0.7 s for a screen-sized frame,
2-6 s for a tall page, measured on a local production build), each level once.

A render reads a frame's html at its width and height and nothing else
(renderBrowser.loadFrameInto). So a picture now also records a design key, a
SHA-1 of those three, and is current when its version or its design key
matches the frame's (thumbs.shows). The key is computed only while the
frame's html is in memory (an open canvas), and once per frame version. The
version sidecar becomes '<version> <design key>'; a sidecar from before reads
as a version alone, as it did. A picture found current by its design gets
the new version recorded, in memory and in its sidecar, so later requests and
a restart need no hash.
